<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jonesborough, Tennessee</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1779">1779</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jonesborough is a town in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>, founded in 1779 by North Carolina as the seat of Washington County and the oldest town in Tennessee. In 1784 delegates of the western counties met there and declared the State of Franklin, which made it its first seat of government. Its preserved historic centre now hosts the National Storytelling Festival.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jonesborough-tennessee.jpg" alt="Jonesborough, Tennessee" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brian Stansberry · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Washington-county-courthouse-tn1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jonesborough is the county seat of Washington County in the far north-eastern corner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>, and part of the Johnson City metropolitan area, within the Tri-Cities region. It lies where the watershed of the Watauga River meets that of the Nolichucky River, among the low hills and elongated ridges of the Appalachian Ridge-and-Valley Province. The main crest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains">Appalachians</a> rises a few miles to the south-east. It was named after the North Carolina legislator Willie Jones, who had supported the state's westward expansion across the Appalachian Mountains, and for a time it was spelled Jonesboro.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Settlers of European origin founded the town in 1779, seventeen years before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a> became a state, while the area was under North Carolina. It was first part of the Washington District. In 1784 it became the capital of the autonomous State of Franklin, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a>. Congress never recognised Franklin, and North Carolina reclaimed it in late 1788. In 1788 the future president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a> spent several months practising law in Jonesborough, lodging in a cabin of Major Christopher Taylor.</p><p>The town was a centre of abolitionism. The Tennessee Manumission Society was founded in 1815, and in 1819 Elihu Embree of Jonesborough began the Manumission Intelligencier, renamed The Emancipator the next year, the first American periodical devoted only to the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. In the 1840s William G.</p><p>Brownlow published the Jonesborough Whig here, and in May 1840 he brawled in the street with the rival editor Landon Carter Haynes. In the summer of 1873 cholera reached the town. Of about 75 people who remained, 30 died by early August and 30 more fell ill but survived. The victims included the publisher George E. Gresham, who had cared for the sick.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Jonesborough styles itself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>'s oldest town, and its 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> population was 5,860. Its historic district was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1969. The Chester Inn, built in 1797, still stands downtown, and the Christopher Taylor House, built in 1777, was moved into the district and rebuilt there in 1974. The International Storytelling Center holds the National Storytelling Festival on the first full weekend in October, drawing on Appalachian traditions of storytelling.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jonesborough, Tennessee” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Settlers who felt neglected met at <b>Jonesborough</b> and declared a separate state, named after Benjamin Franklin, with the Revolutionary War officer John Sevier as governor.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-franklin">State of Franklin</a></li></ul></section>
